\chapter{Virtual Memory Architecture}

\section{Decoupled Metadata Management}

Traditional monolithic operating systems allocate static arrays of page descriptors proportional to total physical RAM, resulting in significant memory overhead on high-capacity server systems. Telix addresses this by adapting the \emph{Morsel-Driven} allocation paradigm originally developed for high-throughput database query engines \footcite{kemp14}.

Physical memory is partitioned into elastic, self-contained memory blocks ("morsels"). Instead of linear page descriptor lookup tables, translation mapping overhead is decoupled from address space size through software-managed inverted and clustered page tables \footcite{tall95, huck93}:

\begin{equation}
T_{\texttt{lookup}}(\texttt{VA}) = H(\texttt{VPN}) \pmod{N_{\texttt{buckets}}}
\end{equation}

Where $H(\texttt{VPN})$ is a hashed virtual page number mapping to a fixed-size bucket array, ensuring sublinear metadata bounds $O(M)$ where $M$ is the active working set rather than total physical capacity $P$.

\section{Superpaging and Page Clustering}

To minimize Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) misses, Telix uses transparent superpage allocation \footcite{nava02} backed by shared page table hierarchies across co-located threads \footcite{ko03}. Physical allocation utilizes page clustering algorithms derived from BSD VM extensions \footcite{baba79} and Linux kernel VM optimizations \footcite{dick01} to preserve contiguous physical memory pools.
